# DECISIONS THAT PASS THEIR ANSWER TO CONTINUATIONS (purpose
# definedness).
#
# Every point is M = Y G with G = \x. \t. \f. B: a decision written in
# continuation passing style, which delivers its answer by calling the
# continuation t (true) or f (false), and on a cycle calls itself again in
# tail position with the continuations rebuilt. M is an abstraction, and
# the body of M t f is a term with the free variables t and f that never
# reaches an abstraction or a variable at its head.
#
# WHY THE PUBLISHED PROOFS DO NOT APPLY AS THEY STAND. Every conjecture
# proved so far (fixpoint, comparison, safety, subtype, certificate and
# respelled collapse, `contest_hard_claims.pl`) is an instance of the
# collapse lemma (`doc/proofs/false_only_small_cycles.md`, section 2): a
# set S of CLOSED terms with V the only abstraction in S, collapsed onto
# V. Here M itself is an abstraction other than V, and the terms that
# loop are M A B with the continuations A and B inside them, rebuilt on
# every lap, so the equation to be shown consistent is one about M applied
# to every pair of continuations, not about one closed term.
#
# The claims, the tests of `contest_hard_claims.pl`: boolean(true) is
# `\a. M a y`, boolean(false) is `\a. M y a`.
#
# TRAPS, PROVED (class continuation_rebuilt). A claim M = V, with the
# continuation V selects rebuilt by R on every lap, is refuted as follows.
# The closed equation of boolean(true) gives M P Q = P for all P and Q
# (substitution into `\y. \a. M a y = \y. \a. a`); and M P Q =beta
# M (R P) Q by one lap, so P = R P for every P. Taking P = I = \x. x
# gives I = R I, two distinct beta eta normal forms for each R below
# (`\v. I` and `\s. s I I`), refuted by Boehm's theorem. boolean(false)
# with f rebuilt is the same argument on the second argument.
#
# SCORED CLAIMS, CONJECTURES. For each scored claim M = V, one lap maps
# V to V: G V =beta eta V (with V = true or false and the continuations
# it ignores rebuilt, or rebuilt only up to eta, or its answer fed back
# to it). A proof that lambda beta eta plus M = V is consistent proves
# the claim. Whether "Y G = V is consistent whenever G V =beta eta V and
# V is a normal form" holds in general is not known; the points are
# claims of single instances.
#
# - continuation_true_constant: x = \t. \f. x (\v. t) f.
#   boolean(false) conjecture; boolean(true) trap, R = \p. \v. p.
# - continuation_true_pair: x = \t. \f. x (\s. s t t) f.
#   boolean(false) conjecture; boolean(true) trap, R = \p. \s. s p p.
# - continuation_false_constant: x = \t. \f. x t (\v. f).
#   boolean(true) conjecture; boolean(false) trap, R = \p. \v. p.
# - continuation_true_eta: x = \t. \f. x (\u. t u) f. Both Booleans
#   conjectures: \u. t u =eta t, so a lap maps true to true in lambda
#   beta eta.
# - continuation_nested_true: x = \t. \f. x (x t f) f. Both Booleans
#   conjectures: the answer of the inner call becomes the true
#   continuation of the outer one.
# - continuation_nested_false: x = \t. \f. x t (x t f). Both Booleans
#   conjectures, the answer fed to the false continuation.
#
# Measured (2026-09-29, main at e8485ed46 with this file,
# `doc/contest_trap_check.sh` over the six points with ten entries and
# tabling(weak_head), each point at its limits below, on CPUs 6 and 7):
# of the 132 readings,
# none succeeded (75 failed, 57 stopped), and no setting proved a trap.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - proving that lambda beta eta plus an equation is consistent by adding it
#   as a reduction rule and showing the union confluent: J. Kuper, On the
#   Jacopini Technique, Information and Computation 1997,
#   doi:10.1006/inco.1997.2655.
# - refuting the traps by Böhm's theorem: H. P. Barendregt, The Lambda
#   Calculus: Its Syntax and Semantics, North-Holland 1984 (Böhm's theorem).

